          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have been tired and light-headed for months, my heart races on stairs, and my periods are heavy. Is that urgent, what could iron deficiency do to the blood, and which ICD-10 chapter covers anemia?",
          "answer": "**Is this urgent?** The urgency check returned routine, and no emergency pattern matched. That is not a clearance. Light-headedness and a racing heart on exertion are worth a prompt visit to a clinician, not a wait-and-see approach. Book an appointment soon, and tell the clinician all of these symptoms together. Because they have lasted for months, the consultation reference suggests seeing a clinician within the next few days.\n\nSeek emergency care now if you faint or nearly faint, have chest pressure or tightness (especially spreading to the arm, jaw, or back, or with breathlessness or sweating), have severe breathlessness at rest, or have a heartbeat that feels very fast or irregular and does not settle (red-flag-chest-pain). The consultation reference also lists soaking through pads quickly, passing black or tarry stools, or vomiting blood as reasons for immediate assessment, because they can mean bleeding from the gut (red-flag-gastrointestinal-bleeding).\n\n**What iron deficiency can do:** Iron helps make the red blood cells that carry oxygen. When iron stores run low, the blood can become anemic. Common effects include tiredness, breathlessness on exertion, palpitations, dizziness, headache, pale skin, cold hands and feet, and brittle nails and hair thinning (condition-iron-deficiency-anemia). The onset is often gradual and the body compensates for a long time, so people often attribute months of fatigue to other causes. Your symptoms match this pattern, but that does not mean you have it. Only blood tests can show it. A blood count shows the pattern, and ferritin is the main test of iron stores. The sources do not explain how heavy periods lead to iron loss, so ask a clinician about this directly. A deficiency can come from ongoing blood loss that needs its own investigation.\n\n**Other possibilities a clinician would consider:** An underactive thyroid (hypothyroidism) can cause tiredness and heavy or irregular periods. Heart causes such as coronary artery disease or heart failure can cause breathlessness and fatigue on exertion, and a clinician would check these given your racing heart on stairs. Anxiety can cause palpitations and dizziness, but physical causes are usually checked first. None of this means you have any of these conditions.\n\n**ICD-10 chapter:** Anemia falls under Chapter 3, Diseases of the blood, blood-forming organs and the immune mechanism (D50–D89), in the US clinical modification (coding-icd10-chapter-03-blood). Codes D55–D59 cover inherited and acquired forms, and D60–D64 cover bone-marrow failure anemias and anemia linked to another disease. I could not confirm the specific code for iron-deficiency anemia from the sources, so check the classification or ask a clinician or medical coder. Anemia is often a sign of another problem, and chronic blood loss causing iron deficiency is coded from the chapter for the bleeding site. Until a diagnosis is documented, symptoms are coded in Chapter 18 (coding-icd10-chapter-18-symptoms).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
